Protocol: PhotoPrint_W3

# Week 3 version: A authenticates to idp using her password (not as
# encryption key), idp issues a signed Grant token, A forwards it to P,
# P presents it to B together with a nonce for replay protection.
#
# pwd(A) is a shared secret between A and idp.
#
# Na  : nonce freshly generated by A (not in initial knowledge)
# Np  : nonce freshly generated by P (not in initial knowledge)
# Grant: fresh token generated by idp (not in initial knowledge)

Types:
    Agent A, P, B, idp;
    Number Na, Np, Grant;
    Function pwd, photos;

Knowledge:
    A:   A, P, B, idp, pk(idp), pk(B), pk(P), pwd(A);
    idp: A, P, B, idp, pk(idp), inv(pk(idp)), pk(P), pk(B), pwd(A);
    P:   A, P, B, idp, pk(idp), pk(P), inv(pk(P)), pk(B);
    B:   A, P, B, idp, pk(idp), pk(B), inv(pk(B)), pk(P), photos(A)

Actions:
    # Step 1: A authenticates to idp using her password and a fresh nonce Na.
    # Encrypted with idp's public key so the password stays confidential.
    A   -> idp: {A, P, B, pwd(A), Na}pk(idp)

    # Step 2: idp verifies the password, issues a signed Grant token.
    # Signed with idp's private key so any party can verify it came from idp.
    # Includes Na for replay protection (binds the Grant to this session).
    idp -> A:   {A, P, B, Grant, Na}inv(pk(idp))

    # Step 3: A forwards the idp-signed Grant to P, encrypted for P.
    A   -> P:   {{A, P, B, Grant, Na}inv(pk(idp))}pk(P)

    # Step 4: P presents the Grant to B. P encrypts for B and includes
    # a fresh nonce Np so B can provide a fresh response bound to this request.
    P   -> B:   {{A, P, B, Grant, Na}inv(pk(idp)), Np}pk(B)

    # Step 5: B verifies the idp signature on the Grant, then returns
    # the photos encrypted for P, including Np to bind the response to P's request.
    B   -> P:   {photos(A), Np}pk(P)

Goals:
    Grant secret between A, idp, P, B;
    photos(A) secret between B, P;
    P authenticates A on Grant;
    B authenticates A on Grant;
    P authenticates B on Np




# =============================================================================
# Special Task - Week 3: Lazy Intruder — Executability of Role P
# =============================================================================
#
# We instantiate P = i (intruder plays role P) and keep A = a, B = b,
# idp = idp as honest agents. We show that the intruder can send every
# outgoing message of role P having only seen the preceding incoming messages,
# following the lazy intruder method from the lecture.
#
# Role P's steps in the protocol:
#   Receive (step 3): {{a, i, b, Grant, Na}inv(pk(idp))}pk(i)
#   Send   (step 4): {{a, i, b, Grant, Na}inv(pk(idp)), Np}pk(b)
#   Receive (step 5): {photos(a), Np}pk(i)
#
# --- Intruder's initial knowledge ---
#
#   M0 = { a, b, i, idp, pk(a), pk(b), pk(i), inv(pk(i)), pk(idp) }
#   (This corresponds to role P's initial knowledge with P = i, A = a, B = b.)
#
# --- Honest strands running in parallel ---
#
#   Role A (with A = a, P = i):
#     a   -> i   : {a, i, b, pwd(a), n1}pk(idp)        [a sends to i, intercepted]
#     idp -> a   : {a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp))     [idp replies to a; token = fresh Grant]
#     a   -> i   : {{a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp))}pk(i)   [msg_in1: a sends to i = P]
#
#   Role B (with A = a, P = i, B = b):
#     i   -> b   : {{a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp)), n2}pk(b)   [msg_out: P's outgoing]
#     b   -> i   : {photos(a), n2}pk(i)                           [msg_in2: b replies to i = P]
#
# We use: n1 = concrete value of Na (generated by a), n2 = concrete value of Np (chosen by i).
#
# --- Step-by-step lazy intruder execution ---
#
# [Receive step 3]
#   i receives msg_in1 = {{a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp))}pk(i)
#   M1 = M0 ∪ { msg_in1 }
#
#   Analysis: apply DecAsym to msg_in1:
#     requires inv(pk(i)) — M1 ⊢c inv(pk(i))? Yes (inv(pk(i)) ∈ M0).
#     => add inner term: {a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp))  to M1.
#   M1* = M1 ∪ { {a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp)) }
#
#   Analysis: apply OpenSig to {a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp)):
#     OpenSig rule: M ⊢ {m}inv(k) => M ⊢ m (no key needed to open a signature).
#     => add: a, i, b, token, n1  to M1*.
#   M1** = M1* ∪ { a, i, b, token, n1, Grant=token }
#   (Note: a, b, i, idp already in M0; n1 and token are new.)
#
# [Send step 4]
#   i must send: {{a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp)), n2}pk(b)
#   i chooses n2 freshly (i generates its own nonce Np = n2).
#
#   Check M1** ⊢ {{a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp)), n2}pk(b) :
#
#   By Compose (EncAsym with key pk(b)):
#     Need M1** ⊢ pk(b)          — Yes, pk(b) ∈ M0.           [Axiom]
#     Need M1** ⊢ {a,i,b,token,n1}inv(pk(idp)), n2
#       By Compose (pair/concat):
#         Need M1** ⊢ {a,i,b,token,n1}inv(pk(idp))
#           => Yes, ∈ M1*.                                      [Axiom]
#         Need M1** ⊢ n2
#           => n2 is chosen fresh by i (i generates it).        [Fresh/Compose]
#   => M1** ⊢ {{a, i, b, token, n1}inv(pk(idp)), n2}pk(b)      [OK]
#
#   M2 = M1** ∪ { n2 }
#
# [Receive step 5]
#   i receives msg_in2 = {photos(a), n2}pk(i)
#   M3 = M2 ∪ { msg_in2 }
#
#   Analysis: apply DecAsym to msg_in2:
#     requires inv(pk(i)) — M3 ⊢c inv(pk(i))? Yes (∈ M0).
#     => add: photos(a), n2  to M3.
#   M3* = M3 ∪ { photos(a), n2 }   (n2 already in M2)
#
#   => M3* ⊢ photos(a)              [Axiom]
#
# --- Conclusion ---
#
#   The intruder can successfully execute role P:
#     - Step 4 (send): forwards the idp-signed token received from a,
#       bundled with its own fresh nonce n2, encrypted for b. [Axiom + Compose]
#     - Step 5 (receive): decrypts b's response using inv(pk(i)).
#   Role P is executable by the intruder.
#   The intruder learns photos(a) at the end of the execution.
